---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_ACRONYM, SITE_EXPANDED, SITE_LICENSE, SITE_LICENSE_URL } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Methodology, Epistemic Standards & Archiving | ${SITE_ACRONYM}`}
  description={`How ${SITE_ACRONYM} (${SITE_EXPANDED}) verifies historical word origins, eliminates archival OCR traps, and preserves open datasets.`}
>
  <article class="prose-container article-prose" style="padding: 2rem 0;">
    <h1>Methodology, Epistemic Ladder & Open Archiving</h1>
    <p>
      <strong>{SITE_ACRONYM} ({SITE_EXPANDED})</strong> is an open-access historical lexicography
      project investigating 1700–1940 English slang, occupational vernacular, maritime jargon, and
      idioms currently marked <em>origin unknown</em>, <em>uncertain</em>, or <em>disputed</em> in
      standard reference dictionaries.
    </p>

    <h2>Two-Stage Monograph → Case File Workflow</h2>
    <p>
      Every {SITE_ACRONYM} investigation runs through a two-stage pipeline. First, our research swarm
      compiles an exhaustive archival monograph and a machine-validated JSON ledger auditing dozens
      of candidate citations, negative-corpus baselines, and rival donor lexicons. Second, each
      completed monograph is distilled into a readable {SITE_ACRONYM} Case File here—pairing zoomed
      physical-ink proof plates with calibrated prose that separates hard chronological facts from
      explicit linguistic hypotheses.
    </p>

    <h2>The Three-Gate Verification Funnel & Archival Traps A–D</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>PRIMARY-SCAN-VERIFIED:</strong> Original archival page scan and printed issue
        masthead/title page visually inspected character by character, ruling out bound-serial
        metadata misdating (<strong>Trap A</strong>), broken-type OCR ghosts (<strong>Trap B</strong>),
        proper-noun/surname homographs (<strong>Trap C</strong>), and modern editorial wrappers
        (<strong>Trap D</strong>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>PHONOLOGICAL-HYPOTHESIS:</strong> Documented historical dialect, occupational, or
        contact-language donor pathway grounded in pre-cutoff primary lexicons and socio-historical
        contact geography, explicitly labeled where an intermediate oral step remains unattested.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>FALSIFIED-MYTH:</strong> Popular folk etymologies or raw digital search mirages
        disproved by primary chronology or physical-ink inspection, published openly so fellow
        researchers do not repeat dead-end searches.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Open Licensing, Archiving & Citation Policy</h2>
    <p>
      All {SITE_ACRONYM} case files, composite proof plates, BibTeX records, and JSON verification
      ledgers are published under the{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LICENSE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International ({SITE_LICENSE})
      </a>{' '}
      license. Full-text content is syndicated via our <a href="/rss.xml">RSS 2.0 feed</a>, tagged
      with Highwire Press (<code>citation_*</code>) and Schema.org (<code>ScholarlyArticle</code>)
      metadata for Google Scholar and Zotero, and paired with Zenodo / Knowledge Commons data
      deposits for long-term DOI preservation.
    </p>

    <h2>AI-Assisted Archival Search & Human Verification Statement</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE_ACRONYM} uses large language models and deterministic Python harnesses for dirty-OCR
      query expansion, prosopographical cross-indexing, and structured ledger validation across
      public-domain newspaper, patent, courtroom, and periodical archives. Every cited primary scan,
      masthead date, leaf number, and diplomatic quotation published on this site is verified against
      unmodified archival page images, with direct links to the host repositories so any reader can
      audit the ink firsthand.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
